: {"margin": 14.7, "lean": 3.0, "environment": 8.2, "incumbency": 2.0, "money": 1.5}, "rating_margin": 12.5, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.76, "baseline_margin": 13.38, "analyst_adjustment": -0.88, "key_factors": ["Strong expert consensus, with most raters at Likely or Safe D", "Tran's overwhelming fundraising edge (~$5.0M raised, $3.3M on hand vs. Vo's $0.5M)", "Markets price Tran near 94% in both Kalshi and Polymarket", "Freshman incumbent in a redrawn, D-leaning district with no polling available"], "rationale": "Raters, prediction markets, and fundamentals all point the same way: Tran holds a large money advantage and a favorable district lean, while Vo has raised little. With no general-election polling, uncertainty stays elevated, but the evidence supports a Safe D rating.", "watch": "Any late independent polling showing Vo within single digits, or a surge of outside spending against Tran, would warrant moving this toward Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Derek Tran (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Freshman Democrat Derek Tran seeks a second term in California's redrawn 45th District against Republican Chuong Vo. The district leans Democratic by about three points, and Tran enters with a decisive financial advantage, having raised roughly five million dollars and holding over three million on hand, while Vo has raised under half a million with modest reserves. Outside spending has been negligible on both sides.\n\nNo general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals and expert judgment. Most race raters place the contest at Likely or Safe D, and prediction markets give Tran roughly a 94 percent chance of winning. The race is rated Safe Democratic, with Tran favored by a double-digit margin, though the absence of polling leaves some room for error.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9684, "margin": 12.49}, {"